hi there Steve coffin here how do we get  to speak in a language I'm sure those of  you who have been following my youtube  channel and who have read my blog and  are on link realize that I believe that  input based learning is very important I  believe in developing a large passive  vocabulary focusing on comprehension a  lot of listening and reading I believe  that you know a large passive vocabulary  is your key sort of foundation for  becoming fluent in a language but fluent  means speaking so how do you get how do  you convert that passive knowledge into  the ability to speak well and I  regularly hear from people who say that  they can read well and understand but  they can't speak well I want to get into  that subject today first of all though  very often when people say that they  read well that doesn't necessarily mean  that they read enough I think we should  all get to the stage where we can  actually read novels in another language  I mean that's ideal now not everyone has  the luxury you know to spend the time to  get to where they can read novels so but  let's get now to this central issue of  how do we start speaking and how do we  become good at speaking in and what we  can what can we do to speed up the  process first thing is you have to kind  of be willing to break through what I  call the stupidity barrier it's like a  sound barrier but it's the barrier of  sounding stupid you have to be willing  to sound stupid and you sound stupid  more to yourself than to the person  listening to you because you're aware of  the fact that you're mumbling and  mangling the language and not speaking  it as well as you would like and not you  know your  thoughts and the feelings aren't coming  out the way you would like and it's kind  of discouraging and at times it seems as  if you've made a bit of progress in the  next time you go to speak you feel as if  you've slipped and become worse again  and and so I mean and and all I can say  is that everyone experiences this I  experienced it and you know we have that  first exhilarating feeling as as we're  able to say a few things in the language  and then to get from there to where we  are generally genuinely fluent and able  to express ourselves is just a long long  road and we have to be willing to put  ourselves in the situation where we  stumble and say the wrong thing and we  have to in many cases we have to trust  our instincts and trust our intuition  just go for it and say it and if it  sounds stupid it doesn't matter if we  get too hung up about trying to speak  perfectly that often inhibits us so so  basically again just to review what I've  said there was I will still believe in a  major emphasis on input lots of reading  getting to the point if possible where  you can read novels make sure you can  understand because that's your basic  defensive position if you're speaking to  someone and you don't understand what  they're saying  that's very unsettling if you can get to  where you can at least understand what  they're saying now you're into the stage  where you're stumbling and mumbling and  trying to say something in the language  yourself and so there you have to be  prepared to go forward with whatever you  have don't worry if it's right or wrong  just go for it generally it sounds a lot  better than you think you tend to feel  that you're bad the person listening to  you I've seen this time and time again  actually is has no trouble communicates  understands and and recognizes that of  course that you're not a native speaker  but thinks you're doing a lot better  than mostly you think you're doing okay  so you have to be willing to go through  that stage now what are the things we  can do to help us along okay some people  they talk to yourself and there's  nothing wrong with that I've always  found it difficult to maintain that I  might say you know five phrases to  myself during the day but but I'm not  gonna do that as an ongoing thing I'm  not gonna stand some people stand in  front of a mirror and talk and I'm sure  that's a good thing to do but it's not  something that I could sustain writing  is an excellent thing to do since I'm  not enrolled in a language program and I  don't have assignments to hand in I  don't write because I'm quite frankly  too lazy to write but if you can if you  have the discipline I think it's an  excellent thing to do and there are many  sites including link or you can put up  some writing and people are quite happy  to come and and correct it the thing to  remember is that the the major benefit  of writing is the exercise of writing  itself but the corrections are also  useful because you could be you know  stuck in a certain pattern which is not  sort of normal usage and it pointed out  to you that no this is not normal usage  the easiest thing to do is to talk and  so now for example I oh yeah  Russian it turns out you know I've been  listening to echo muskie for six years  one of the people who appears regularly  on echo muskie is my vagin a Polsky he  is in Vancouver he's giving a talk on  Thursday to a group of Russian people I  have bought a ticket are gonna be there  I'm gonna meet one of the guys that I've  been listening to for six years so I've  been working on improving my Russian and  so I've been doing a lot of talking I  mean to speak well you have to speak a  lot so I've been signing up for  discussions with our tutors at link  Evgeny in st. Petersburg Elena in Kiev  or near Kiev and in the Ukraine and then  there's Vladimir who is a Russian who  lives in Winnipeg who's also a tutor at  Lincoln he's a great guy so I've been  talking a lot talking is good you  discover your faults I get feedback in  the form of reports or even during my  discussion and that's very good as an  example I was used to saying you know  Patamon store button will stop because  in situations where in fact in Russian  it's not because it's post cold clue  it's more like or tack karke  etc it's more like since rather than  because and in different circumstances  some of these connecting words are used  differently which Lada Mir pointed out  to me which was very useful now one way  of getting instant feedback which I  started doing and I I just mentioned it  to you and that is I had in the past  experimented with using Google Translate  to get sort of an instant translation of  what I am trying to say because if I can  struggle and try and say it myself but  it takes a while and I'm too lazy to  write but now I've got this tremendous  thing because on my Mac there is  dictation it's tremendous I got  dictation in English in French in  Spanish in German in Japanese in Korean  so first of all I don't even have to  change my keyboard to write in these  languages I just dictate it and it's you  know it prints out for me I've got the  text in front of me which is tremendous  but the way I'm now starting to use it  is I go to Google Translate and I  dictate English into the English side  and I've set it up to go from English to  Russian and then the Russian shows up  and so I just deliberately try to say  things that I would like to say of  course the Russian is a bit clumsy it's  not all natural but a lot of the key  phrases in fact are very accurate  because all of these you know machine  translation systems get better and  better at translating things based on  context I'm sure it's better for some  languages than for others but for  Russian it's really quite good so then I  take the resulting whatever I have you  know said which has been converted into  Russian I take it I imported into link I  save some of the key phrases I can still  remember what it was what it was that I  had wanted to say so in a way it's a way  of speaking or at least picking up off  on some of these key phrases that you  need to speak in other words something  that you  wanted to say in English into Russian so  I mean that's just a minor thing the  main thing is just to do a lot of  talking  so to summarize on this whole issue of  how we get to speaking some people say  you should speak from the beginning  which is fine if you have lots of time I  have an hour so a day most of it while  driving my car or exercising I can't  surround myself with people with whom I  can speak Russian so I prefer her to  focus on a lot of input activity to  build up my familiarity with the  language as I've said many times read  and save words on link and stuff like  that but now that I want to speak  Russian better ultimately I have to  speak more and when I speak I just let  it all hang out and go for it and when I  go to listen to my take on a post key on  Thursday and there will be lots of  Russians in the room and I expect to  engage in discussions on politics or  life in Canada and I will just let it  all hang out and I'll get my cases wrong  and I'll get my head whatever verbs  wrong and it won't matter and the more I  can expose myself to that kind of  situation  the sooner I will improve so there you  have it just a bit of a take on getting  to speaking thank you for listening bye  for now
 

